The system must first understand what makes the "best bits" of a project actually work. Instead of scraping raw text, specialized agents deconstruct repositories into reusable logic blocks.The Scavenger Agent: Scans trending or highly-starred repositories across specific domains, pulling down codebases to analyze their structures.The Architect Agent: Extracts the core patterns. It analyzes how the best projects map their Docker container networks, structure their Flask API endpoints, or modularize their React and Tailwind CSS components.The Knowledge Base: The extracted architectural blueprints are stored in a centralized markdown-based vault—effectively an Obsidian brain for the AI—giving the system a persistent, searchable memory of how top-tier software is structured.Phase 2: The Synthesis EngineThis is where the system generates something unseen. Rather than copying existing tools, the AI cross-pollinates concepts that do not usually intersect.The Innovator Agent: Queries the knowledge base to combine disparate patterns. For example, it might fuse the gamified logic from one repository with the interactive SVG and JavaScript radial interfaces of a system dashboard from another, outputting a blueprint for a completely novel platform.Constraint Injection: You feed the system specific prompts via a CLI tool like Claude Code, steering the Innovator's wilder ideas into functional, cohesive project architectures.Phase 3: Autonomous Assembly & ExecutionOnce the novel blueprint is created, the system must build and prove it works without human hand-holding.The Builder Agent: Writes the synthesized code and immediately spins it up. It generates the docker-compose.yml files, builds the images, and launches the application in an isolated local network.The QA Loop: If a container crashes or a frontend component fails to render, the agent reads the runtime logs, patches the code, and redeploys autonomously until the application is stable.Running the Test on Distributed HardwareTo make this test truly stand out, the environment itself can be part of the architecture. Instead of running everything on a single machine, the multi-agent system can be distributed across a physical home server cluster.By utilizing a network of single-board computers—such as Raspberry Pi 4s or Compute Modules linked through a network switch—each node can handle a distinct piece of the pipeline. One node runs the Scavenger, another handles the Synthesis engine, and a third hosts the live Docker containers of the newly generated applications, creating a tangible, physical manifestation of the AI brain.